  "metadata": {
    "title": "Hashtree: Content-Addressed, Encrypted, Peer-to-Peer Storage with Nostr-Anchored Mutable References — An Architectural and Comparative Analysis",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22761375",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-15",
    "description": "<p>Centralized content delivery networks and cloud object stores concentrate publication, authentication, and<br>distribution in a small number of operators, and the global outages of 2021&ndash;2025 demonstrated how quickly that<br>concentration propagates failure to the application layer. This paper presents an architectural and comparative<br>analysis of Hashtree, an MIT-licensed storage system that couples content-addressed Merkle trees with<br>content-hash-key (CHK) encryption, Nostr-anchored mutable references of the form npub/tree/path, and<br>peer-to-peer distribution over the Federated Interoperable Peering System (FIPS), WebRTC data channels, and<br>Blossom blob servers. Using documentary analysis of specifications, package registries, and the reference<br>implementation, the study reconstructs the write path, the per-block read-path cascade, and the authorization<br>model, and situates Hashtree within the lineage of Freenet/Hyphanet, Venti, Git, IPFS, and BitTorrent v2. The<br>analysis suggests that the design delivers meaningful properties &mdash; end-to-end verification,<br>deduplication-compatible encryption, and self-certifying addressing that requires no DNS, certificate authorities,<br>or accounts &mdash; while inheriting known limitations of convergent-style encryption, depending on unremunerated<br>seeding for availability, and deferring content governance to server operators and social-graph heuristics. The<br>demand-scaled distribution claims appear plausible for popular content but not for the long tail. The paper<br>contributes a structured comparative matrix, a threat-and-availability assessment, and a research agenda spanning<br>measurement, formal transport verification, and privacy-preserving deduplication.</p>",
